  "notes": "The Invisible Men member who co-produced both *Come Over When You're Sober* albums — documented as \"[helping] sandpaper [the songs] into shape\", the professional-studio counterweight to Smokeasac's SoundCloud instincts. He shares the grid (four-note minor motif, 808 under guitars, triple-time hats) so this file's job is to author the *difference*, and every parameter below is one of the four differences the research names. (1) \"Arranged in conventional pop sections with cleaner transitions; drums enter on the downbeat of a section rather than mid-phrase\" — `quantizeStrength` 0.97, `switchUpProb` 0.06 and `dropOutBeats` down to 0.3, against Smokeasac's 0.65, plus a verse/pre/chorus scaffolding in `structures`. (2) \"A lifted b7→1 or 5→6 move at the end of the hook to give a chorus its resolution\" — `borrowed.V` at 0.25 with `harmonic_minor` weighted into the scale pool, because a raised leading tone is what that lift is. (3) \"A second guitar layer and vocal harmony stacks that the pure-SoundCloud version lacks\" — `countermelody.densityRatio` 0.55 with `arp` and `answer_lick`, well above his co-producer's 0.35. (4) \"808 root doubling the guitar, sidechained under the vocal so the top line stays intelligible\" — the 808 velocity tier drops and `chordToneBias` rises to 0.74 so the top line resolves. ⚠ `medium` matches the research, and the honesty warning in the paragraph is unusually strong: his role and credits are documented, but **the bullets separating his contribution from Smokeasac's are themselves inference**, marked `[LOW-CONF]`. So the INFERRED parameters here are the borrowed-V lift, the drum-entry and sidechain readings, and the tempo — that is most of this file, and it is only defensible as *the pop-craft delta on the Smokeasac grid*.",
